From 987945eb78b5acd96dc955e6354f5deb58264b2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ember arlynx <ember@lunar.town>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 13:32:51 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] tower256: a fast work-9104 prover and its CUDA port,
 byte-identical to the conformance kernel

prover/src/tower256_fri_fast.rs replaces the conformance kernel's three loops that run above
the counts in docs/TOWER256-FRI-PROVER-WORK.md, without changing the receipt bytes:
- encode: a Gao-Mateer additive FFT of the monomial word on offset + span(beta), for any basis.
  Every subproblem at one depth shares its basis and offset, so each depth needs one inversion.
  A dependent basis falls back to Horner.
- Merkle: every tree level is stored once, and openings read their siblings from it.
- domains: each round map is GF(2)-linear, so a domain is the XOR span of its images. Each fold
  is one multiply per point.
Arithmetic runs in a polynomial basis reached by the field isomorphism y -> y(omega), with
PMULL/PCLMULQDQ and Barrett reduction. Hashes are a raw Keccak-f from the cSHAKE midstates.

tests/tower256_fri_fast_conformance.rs checks byte equality on the five Lean vectors. It also
checks 30 random requests against the conformance kernel (any basis, offsets, m and q, words
longer than the domain, a dependent basis), the production shape at ell 8 and 10, and the
additive FFT against Horner.

src/bin/tower256_fri_fast_bench.rs reports timings by phase and writes the device check data.

prover/cuda/: Keccak leaves and nodes from the midstate, Tower256 Karatsuba down to GF(2^8)
with log/exp tables in shared memory, norm inversion, the additive FFT pieces, the fold and the
final interpolation. tower256_gpu.py is the cupy host. The same .cu also builds as host C++
(-DHOST_EMU), and that build is bit-exact on the Lean vectors and on ell 8..20.

The lib.rs line is a proposal to the Tower256 lane.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
